// Package platform/accessibility talks to a screen reader beyond what
// views say themselves: it has it read a text (what changed where its
// focus isn't), and moves its focus to a view.
//
//   accessibility.announce("Saved")   // a screen reader wouldn't notice the toast
//   accessibility.focus(error)        // error: a Ref of the view to read next
//
// Without a screen reader on, both do nothing. What a view says (its
// label, its role, its value) is the view's own: its a11y props.

/// Has the screen reader, if one is on, read text: a toast, a count of
/// search results, a save that finished. UIAccessibility's announcement
/// on iOS, announceForAccessibility on Android, an announcement request
/// on macOS, a UI Automation notification on Windows, GTK's announcement
/// on Linux (GTK 4.14 and later), a polite aria-live region on the web.
/// Don't announce what the focused view says already.
pub native fn announce(text: string)

/// Moves the screen reader's focus to the view (a Ref of it), for when
/// the screen changed under the user and what matters is somewhere else:
/// the error under a form that failed, a search's first result, the
/// title of a panel that slid in. It's sent when the screen next updates,
/// once the view exists and has its frame; a Ref that holds no view does
/// nothing.
pub native fn focus(view: Ref)
